</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     INTRODUCTION SECTION
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
  <h2 id="intro-heading" class="sr-only">About AEST to EST Timezone Converter</h2>
  <p>
    Welcome to our dedicated <strong>AEST to EST Converter</strong> and timezone reference guide. Converting between Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST, UTC+10) and US Eastern Standard Time (EST, UTC−5) is one of the most common international scheduling tasks, bridging major commercial hubs including Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, New York, Toronto, and Washington D.C.
  </p>
  <p>
    Under standard winter civil time in both hemispheres, <strong>AEST is exactly 15 hours ahead of EST</strong>. However, because Australia is located in the Southern Hemisphere and North America is in the Northern Hemisphere, their daylight saving time (DST) schedules operate on opposite calendar cycles. When New York shifts to Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC−4), the time difference shrinks to <strong>14 hours</strong>. When Sydney and Melbourne enter Australian Eastern Daylight Time (AEDT, UTC+11) and the US returns to EST, the time difference expands to <strong>16 hours</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether you are coordinating cross-ocean team handovers, monitoring Wall Street equity openings from the Australian Securities Exchange (ASX), scheduling international flights, or organizing video calls across the Pacific, this resource delivers instant calculations, comprehensive 24-hour schedules, and precise calendar day rollover tracking.
  </p>
</section>

